From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:50:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: decode to SAND on the host, detiled by Mesa
 (opt-in)

With host-nv12 the device model asks the RPi4 bcm2835-codec for linear
NV12 and converts it to the RGBA the guest asked for on the GPU. The
codec is faster when it writes its native NV12_COL128 (SAND,
128-byte-wide columns) format: per the code comment, 1080p60 H.264
decodes at 89 fps into SAND and at 66 fps into NV12 (measured
2026-10-05).

Add a host-sand property. When it is on, S_FMT/TRY_FMT for an RGBA
CAPTURE queue ask the codec for NV12_COL128 instead of NV12. If the
codec refuses, the request is retried with NV12 and the queue runs as
plain host-nv12. For a SAND queue the device remembers the column height
(which the codec reports in bytesperline). It then imports the Y and
CbCr planes as EGL images with the
DRM_FORMAT_MOD_BROADCOM_SAND128_COL_HEIGHT modifier. Mesa v3d accepts
that modifier for R8/GR88 only as external textures and detiles them
itself. A second copy of the NV12 shader samples them through
samplerExternalOES. The visible luma rows are taken as col_height * 2 /
3 (1620 -> 1080 for a 1080p frame).

Only the GPU path can read SAND. When it is not available, the CPU copy
path skips the frame and warns once instead of copying tiled bytes. A
SAND import also needs EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import_modifiers.

host-sand needs host-nv12 and gpu-xfer and defaults to off.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on this layer's virtio-media device and RPi4 codec]
